ation, model configuration, and\nCD3 tests. Weight encode defaults to CPU; CUDA is used only to stage completed\ncompressed buffers and for decode/fused-MM. The legacy GPU encoder is available\nonly through the explicit debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\nUnrelated working-tree changes were intentionally excluded.\n\nThe CPU KV encoder additionally contains the 2026-09-01 optimization set:\n\n- block-owner, single-source-pass encoding;\n- min/max exception-free fast path;\n- SVE2 predicate counting on the exception path;\n- NEON hierarchical packing of 16 three-bit codes;\n- uninitialized temporary exception scratch.\n\nDirect-to-persistent Packed20 packing was measured but not retained because it\nwas 3–6% slower for medium and large batches.\n\nThe consolidated snapshot additionally contains:\n\n- host-native CD3 weight encoding with 64-row stripe parallelism;\n- adaptive OpenMP team sizing for a single KV compression worker;\n- batched NumPy KV slot allocation instead of a per-block Python loop;\n- production fused-MM crossover calibration through N=128 on GH200.\n- 128-thread standalone CD3 decode, with two logical chunks per thread;\n- two half-width live output accumulators with the same two 128-bit stores,\n  reducing standalone decode latency without changing the format;\n- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV decode launch geometry;\n- direct-to-cache indexed KV decode with no raw-BF16 intermediate copy;\n- selected-run KV staging for partial restores below the slot high-water mark.\n- one aligned slot-major CD3 blob and row-stride-aware CPU/GPU kernels;\n- one `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` submission for all K/V staging runs;\n- cached block/slot device metadata and one native K/V decode dispatch.\n- active-set identity/content caching with generation-safe invalidation;\n- a dense steady-state decode plan that skips filtering, sorting and run scans.\n- a fully CPU P4Flat weight post-process (blocked relayout and subtile codec),\n  so CPU weight encoding no 